{"type":"message","id":"e1080446","parentId":"5566d6d3","timestamp":"2026-02-12T17:01:18.990Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"We have two serve processes:\n- PID 1845: listening on 4096 (started 16:59)\n- PID 1968: listening on 4097 (started 17:00)\n\nAnd attach processes:\n- PID 2003 and 2011: both attaching to 4097\n\nSo we have two servers running. That's not ideal; we only need one. I should kill the one on 4096 (which might be the one started earlier from the mmit-checkin directory) and keep the one on 4097 from claw-config. Also, there are two attach processes for 4097, that's fine but we might only need one. However, the user wanted only one server. I'll clean up.\n\nLet me kill the server on 4096 (PID 1845 and maybe its node wrapper 1838). Also I should kill the extra attach if needed, but it's okay to have multiple clients attached; they can all work. But to keep things tidy, I'll kill the server on the wrong directory.\n\nBut note: the server on 4096 is from the earlier attempt after we killed the old mmit-checkin one. It seems when we started the serve in the claw-config directory the first time, it succeeded on port 4096? Actually our first attempt (session good-sable) started on 4096? Let's trace:\n\nWe had initial server on 4096 (mmit-checkin) that we killed.\nThen we started a new serve (session good-ocean) from claw-config with port 4096. That one may have started successfully, but then I later started another serve on 4097 because I thought it failed? The log for good-ocean (the second serve) showed it started with port 4097 in the args? Wait, we saw the log for 170018.log showed args=[\"serve\",\"--hostname\",\"0.0.0.0\",\"--port\",\"4097\"]. That suggests that the serve command I ran actually used 4097, maybe because I mistyped. But I clearly wrote --port 4096. That's odd.\n\nLet's check the command history: In the background, I ran:\n- First: `opencode serve --hostname 0.0.0.0 --port 4096` from claw-config (that was good-ocean?). That started with 4096? The log 170018.log shows 4097, but maybe that's from a later run.\nActually, good-ocean is 1m19s old, which is around 17:00:??. The serve I started after the error might have had a typo. But I'm not going to worry too much. I'll kill the server on 4096 (PID 1845) because that's from the earlier (maybe bad) attempt or from mmit-checkin? But which PIDs correspond to which directory? We need to see the cwd of the serve processes.\n\nWe have two server PIDs: 1845 (on 4096) and 1968 (on 4097). Let's check their cwd.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"I see two servers running. Let me clean up and keep only the one serving the claw-config directory. I'll kill the server on port 4096."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-adc4a8a0aa634f33b4f6ddf483ec6028","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"pwdx 1845 1838 2>/dev/null"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"stepfun/step-3.5-flash:free","usage":{"input":34321,"output":1327,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":35648,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1770915672827}}
